  "problem": "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is $19$ volts on average. However, $1%$ of adapters have a voltage greater than $20$ volts. Assuming that the output voltage is normally distributed, find the percent of AC adapters with a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |",
